---
/*
 * The inbound half of the interceptor, which is where the protocol's guarantee
 * actually lives. The ticket-granting ticket opens under the TGS's own long-term
 * key; the session key comes out of the ticket; that session key opens the
 * client's authenticator. So the server proves the client holds a key the client
 * never transmitted, and the session key is the accent because it is the piece
 * that was never on the wire.
 *
 * Styling comes from the shared .diagram rules in src/styles/global.css.
 */
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="-8 -6 574 244"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="nested-title nested-desc"
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  >
    <title id="nested-title">Nested decryption of a ticket-granting ticket</title>
    <desc id="nested-desc">
      The client sends a ticket-granting ticket and an authenticator. The interceptor decrypts the
      ticket with the Ticket Granting Service's long-term key, which yields a session key from inside
      the ticket. That session key then decrypts the authenticator. The session key never crosses the
      network, so opening the authenticator proves the client holds it.
    </desc>

    <g class="node-label">
      <text x="0" y="16">what the client sends</text>
    </g>

    <!-- The two things that arrive, each sealed under a different key. -->
    <g class="node">
      <rect x="0" y="26" width="196" height="34" rx="3" />
      <rect x="0" y="72" width="196" height="34" rx="3" />
    </g>
    <g class="field">
      <text x="12" y="48">TGT (sealed)</text>
      <text x="12" y="94">authenticator (sealed)</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Step 1: the TGS opens the ticket with its own long-term key. -->
    <g class="collapse">
      <line x1="204" y1="43" x2="252" y2="43" />
      <polygon points="252,37 266,43 252,49" />
    </g>
    <g class="edge-label">
      <text x="235" y="31" text-anchor="middle">open with</text>
      <text x="235" y="60" text-anchor="middle">TGS key</text>
    </g>

    <g class="node">
      <rect x="276" y="26" width="176" height="34" rx="3" />
    </g>
    <g class="field">
      <text x="288" y="48">ticket contents</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Step 2: the session key is lifted out of the opened ticket. -->
    <g class="accent">
      <line x1="364" y1="60" x2="364" y2="86" />
      <polygon points="358,86 364,100 370,86" />
      <rect x="276" y="104" width="176" height="34" rx="3" />
      <text x="364" y="126" text-anchor="middle">session key</text>
      <text x="452" y="78">never sent</text>
    </g>

    <!-- Step 3: that session key opens the authenticator. -->
    <g class="accent">
      <line x1="276" y1="121" x2="204" y2="121" />
      <polygon points="204,115 190,121 204,127" />
    </g>
    <g class="edge-label">
      <text x="240" y="113" text-anchor="middle">opens</text>
    </g>

    <g class="collapse">
      <line x1="98" y1="112" x2="98" y2="152" />
      <polygon points="92,152 98,166 104,152" />
    </g>

    <g class="node">
      <rect x="0" y="170" width="452" height="34" rx="3" />
    </g>
    <g class="field">
      <text x="12" y="192">so the client holds a key it never transmitted</text>
    </g>

    <g class="edge-label">
      <text x="0" y="226">one interceptor, every KDC endpoint</text>
    </g>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    Inbound, the interceptor decrypts the ticket-granting ticket under the Ticket Granting Service's
    own long-term key, recovers the session key from inside that ticket, and uses it to decrypt the
    client's authenticator. The session key never crosses the network, so the authenticator opening at
    all is the proof that the client holds it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
